In the bustle of the city, we keep a quiet corner for the mind.

We don't chase time; we simply let things grow and settle at their own pace.

Today, two international guests have come from across mountains and seas to experience an authentic Eastern culture of stillness. Tea is an Eastern language that has flowed for a thousand years, carrying the Chinese people's unique composure and elegance. Setting aside elaborate explanations, we let our guests immerse themselves in traditional Chinese tea ritual — feeling the ancient etiquette through which the Chinese cultivate the self and host guests with tea. A single leaf holds a world. This thousand-year-old Eastern serenity, needing no words to explain, crosses borders and lets foreign visitors understand the quiet and grace of Chinese living.

The ancients contemplated the Way through flowers, using the spacing and density of branches to create artistic blank space. We let the vitality of nature fall into a small vessel. Our guests prune, arrange, and shape the branches themselves; in the choosing and discarding of each stem and leaf, they immerse themselves in the beauty of Eastern aesthetics and come to understand the ancient Chinese wisdom of unity between humanity and nature.

If tea is the stillness within, then coffee is warm, exuberant comfort. Grinding the beans for fragrance, controlling the temperature as water pours slowly, watching the coffee drip drop by drop. The same focused stillness, a different mood of life — in the collision of Eastern and Western lifestyles, our international guests come to feel more deeply the essence and power of a slow, mindful life.

Chinese vegetarian cuisine carries the Chinese philosophy of revering nature and returning to simplicity. Stripped of heaviness and complexity, it returns to the purity and comfort of food itself. A vegetarian meal, a taste of simple joy, lets guests from afar know Chinese cuisine through their tongues. An immersive Eastern experience; a gentle cross-cultural encounter.

Many people ask why we insist on keeping such a quiet Eastern space in the noisy city. For us, it is not only a corner where city dwellers can rest their bodies and minds, but also a way to preserve the warmth and vitality of traditional Chinese meditative culture. Using the scenes of everyday life as the medium, we let the ancient culture of Eastern stillness step out of the books, toward the people, toward the world.

This is the original intent of our meditation space: to pass on the Eastern cultural lineage through the smallest rituals of daily life, so that every encounter becomes a gentle act of cultural inheritance — and the beauty of a mindful Chinese way of life is seen, understood, and loved by more people.
